message.content:
{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce that their negotiated agreement 'resulted in the following provisions being included in the Collins-Lieberman legislation' (independence within DHS, direct access to the President, the new agency name). This is a claim of personal responsibility for getting their provisions included in advancing legislation, which the definition and clarification (3) ('their authored/backed provisions were included in advancing... legislation') treat as credit-claiming. It goes beyond merely introducing or advocating a pending bill because the senators claim their intervention produced specific, concrete changes to the bill."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the column praises the U.S. Marshals, the senator explicitly states 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is a direct first-person claim of securing federal funding, squarely within clarification (2) ('I helped secure')."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bond touts that 'the new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This claims personal responsibility (as co-author) for enacted legislation that is delivering concrete federal funds to his state, matching the definition and clarification (5) on touting past enacted achievements. The rest of the release is position-taking, but this claim is a delivered-benefit credit claim."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Senator Shelby's office announces that the Department of Homeland Security will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department in Alabama. Under clarification (1), announcements from the senator's office that federal funds/grants are coming to their state count as credit-claiming because the announcement itself associates the senator with delivering the benefit, even without an explicit 'I secured' statement."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a $105,550 grant to an Ohio fire department 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' states DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for it, and 'passed an updated version.' This combines an announcement of funds coming to his state with explicit claims of authoring/passing legislation and securing funding — core credit-claiming under the definition and clarifications (1), (2), and (5)."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement criticizes Democrats' filibuster of judicial nominees and defends the 'constitutional option.' She takes a position and attacks opponents but claims no governmental action, benefit, or accomplishment for herself. This is classic position-taking under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison signs on as an original cosponsor of a just-introduced veterans benefits bill and says she 'will work with my colleagues to pass this measure.' Under negative clarification (2), cosponsoring or advocating pending legislation without claiming it has advanced or been adopted is not credit-claiming; the promised future effort also falls under negative clarification (4)."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez states his position on the pending Pombo OCS bill — praising Congressman Putnam, objecting to the inadequate buffer, and threatening to join a filibuster. He claims no outcome or accomplishment; this is position-taking on pending legislation under negative clarifications (1) and (2)."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces that $8.1 million for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard was included in the FY2006 MilCon/VA appropriations bill that passed the Senate, noting 'This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' and touts veterans funding she supported and requested via letter to appropriators. This matches the definition (securing/announcing delivered federal funds) and clarification (2) ('at the Senator's request') — a small-delegation claim of delivering a concrete benefit."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release is primarily an event announcement (a Father's Day essay contest), which alone would fall under negative clarification (5). However, it also touts that 'One of Senator Bayh's first pieces of legislation in the Senate was the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into the law by President Bush earlier this year.' This mirrors clarification (5)'s example 'my earlier provisions were signed into law,' which the codebook says counts as credit-claiming 'even when embedded in a release about a new initiative.' I therefore classify it as 1, though the embed is brief and the main purpose is commemorative."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords merely explains his vote in support of the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration's lack of an exit strategy. Under negative clarification (1), explaining a vote and criticizing the administration without claiming any outcome or benefit is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's positive example: Nelson announces that 'his amendment' passed the Senate 72-26 and calls it 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.' Claiming that one's amendment was adopted is explicit credit-claiming under clarification (3)."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy introduces his 'Medicare for All' bill, criticizes the Bush administration and Republicans, and urges colleagues to support the proposal. Introducing and advocating pending legislation without claiming any advance, adoption, or delivered outcome is position-taking under negative clarifications (1) and (2) (mirroring the Kennedy stem-cell negative example)."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release (from Levin's office, with Coleman as PSI Ranking Member) claims concrete results from their subcommittee's oversight: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed... from $680,000 in 2003 to $31 million in 2006,' and levies 'jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' The definition expressly includes 'producing tangible results through oversight,' and clarification (4) covers oversight producing outcomes like back taxes recouped. This is credit-claiming by the subcommittee's leaders."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl co-sponsors an amendment to the Budget Resolution that 'calls for' 2,000 additional Border Patrol agents and funding; there is no claim that the amendment was adopted or advanced. Under negative clarification (2), cosponsoring/advocating pending legislation without claiming it has been adopted is not credit-claiming. The previously passed intelligence reform bill is referenced only as context, with no claim of personal responsibility for it."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release's core action is Cornyn sending a letter requesting that Secretary Chertoff consider a levee proposal — a request/demand of an agency official under negative clarification (4), and he is merely 'hopeful' about future consideration. The background mentions (he 'added an amendment' requiring an IBWC report to Congress and 'sent a letter' requesting appropriations) are past procedural involvement inside a forward-looking advocacy statement where no discrete win (no funds delivered, no agency decision obtained) is being announced; the codebook's final negative sentence says such mentions do not qualify. This was the closest call, but no delivered benefit or adopted-amendment victory is claimed."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release centers on newly introduced legislation (alone insufficient under negative clarification (2)), it also touts a past enacted achievement: Murkowski 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' Claiming to have helped win a concrete funding benefit for Alaska is credit-claiming under clarification (5) (touting past enacted achievements even when embedded in a release about a new initiative) and clarification (2) (informal influence producing a benefit)."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is the codebook's positive example: the announcement of FEMA moving 300 trailers and 100 mobile homes to Mississippi 'follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush,' explicitly tying Lott's personal intervention to the agency's action. Informal influence producing an agency action is credit-claiming under clarification (4)."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison asked questions at a hearing, 'received assurances' that BRAC is on schedule, 'expressed her support' for the Grow the Force request, and said she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood.' Being 'encouraged' by funding without claiming a causal role falls squarely under negative clarification (3) (welcomes/applauds/is 'pleased' or 'encouraged' by an outcome), and 'I have supported increasing the Army's end strength' is general support, not a claim of delivering a specific outcome (negative clarification (7))."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release is forward-looking (urging conferees to retain the provision), it claims a concrete legislative achievement: 'Last month, the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty, and Bayh calls it 'his legislation.' Clarification (3) explicitly includes claims that the senator's amendment was adopted by a chamber, and this is a discrete win rather than mere procedural involvement, so it is not covered by the negative rule on pending legislation (which applies only 'without claiming it has advanced or been adopted')."
  }
}

{
  "50": {
    "document_id": "50",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement is pure criticism of the Bush administration's record on global warming. Per negative clarification (1), merely criticizing the administration and taking a position is not credit-claiming; no concrete benefit or accomplishment is claimed."
  },
  "57": {
    "document_id": "57",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is identical to the codebook's negative example: Kennedy introduces and advocates for stem cell/cloning legislation and urges its passage. Per negative clarification (2), introducing or cosponsoring pending legislation without claiming it has advanced or been adopted is position-taking, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "89": {
    "document_id": "89",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dorgan announces a federal designation (National Power Plant Operations Technology and Educational Center) coming to Bismarck State College and explicitly states he 'authored legislation, which was signed into law, that created the National Center designation.' Per clarification (1) (announcing a designation coming to his state) and (5) (touting a past enacted achievement), this is credit-claiming."
  },
  "240": {
    "document_id": "240",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Ensign merely issues a statement praising Chertoff upon his confirmation and saying he looks forward to working with him. Per negative clarification (1) and (3), this is position-taking/welcoming an outcome with no claim of personal responsibility for any concrete governmental result."
  },
  "453": {
    "document_id": "453",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Nelson explains his votes against two Iraq withdrawal resolutions and advocates setting measurable goals. Per negative clarification (1), explaining a vote and taking a position is not credit-claiming; no delivered outcome is claimed."
  },
  "597": {
    "document_id": "597",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release describes Lugar presenting his Health and Fitness Award to community members at his annual run/walk event. Per negative clarification (5), awards honoring others and event announcements are not credit-claiming; no governmental action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "656": {
    "document_id": "656",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: Kerry's statement on the Dubai ports deal is pure criticism of the Bush administration's port security record. Per negative clarification (1), criticizing the administration without claiming any benefit or accomplishment is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "918": {
    "document_id": "918",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is simply an announcement of a town meeting Allard will hold in Greeley. Per negative clarification (5), event announcements are not credit-claiming; no concrete governmental outcome is claimed."
  },
  "967": {
    "document_id": "967",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline 'SHELBY ANNOUNCES OVER 3 MILLION FOR TALLADEGA' and the text announce HHS releasing $3,305,732 for Head Start programs in Alabama. Per clarification (1), an announcement from the senator's office that federal funds are coming to his state counts as credit-claiming because the announcement itself associates the senator with delivering the benefit."
  },
  "1021": {
    "document_id": "1021",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline 'WYDEN, SMITH GAIN FUNDING APPROVAL' and the release announce that the Senate Appropriations Committee approved funding for the Pacific Coast Salmon Recovery Fund and Oregon State University research. Per clarification (1), announcing federal funds secured/approved for their state counts as credit-claiming, and the 'gain funding approval' framing asserts their role in delivering it."
  },
  "1957": {
    "document_id": "1957",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dodd and Lieberman announce that Bradley International Airport will receive a $2,250,000 federal grant for runway improvements. Per clarification (1), an announcement from the senators' office that federal funds are coming to their state associates them with delivering the benefit and counts as credit-claiming."
  },
  "2318": {
    "document_id": "2318",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as a forward-looking letter urging state action, Durbin explicitly claims in the first person: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding.' These are discrete past wins he claims to have caused, matching clarification (2) (explicit first-person claims like 'I helped secure') and (5) (touting past achievements even in a release about ongoing efforts). This goes beyond the 'past procedural involvement' excluded by the negative clarification because concrete outcomes (an expedited review, restoration to the priority list) are claimed."
  },
  "2807": {
    "document_id": "2807",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lincoln 'applauds' and helps recognize Arkansas Women in Agriculture month, a commemoration/recognition activity under negative clarification (5). The mention that she 'played a key role in the 2002 farm bill debate' is a general description of leadership on an issue under negative clarification (7), not a claim of a specific delivered outcome in this release."
  },
  "3482": {
    "document_id": "3482",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce that Allegany County has been awarded a $294,980 federal SAFER grant. Per clarification (1), an announcement from the senators' office that federal funds/grants are coming to their state counts as credit-claiming because the announcement itself associates the senators with delivering the benefit."
  },
  "3577": {
    "document_id": "3577",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sarbanes's hearing statement criticizes FEMA's implementation of flood insurance reforms and outlines issues for review. His mention that he 'worked with Senator Bunning and others' on the reforms is past procedural involvement embedded in an oversight/forward-looking statement with no discrete win being announced, which the negative clarification explicitly excludes. He also attributes the legislation to Bunning ('Senator Bunning crafted legislation'). Overall this is oversight and criticism, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3659": {
    "document_id": "3659",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: Clinton 'welcomes' the NIOSH advisory recommendation and says she 'will continue to press' for Bethlehem Steel workers. Per negative clarification (3) and (4), applauding an outcome and promising future advocacy without claiming a causal role is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3687": {
    "document_id": "3687",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bingaman announces that New Mexico State University has been awarded $599,691 in USDA funding for food safety research. Per clarification (1), an announcement from the senator's office that federal funds are coming to his state counts as credit-claiming because the announcement itself associates the senator with delivering the benefit."
  },
  "4208": {
    "document_id": "4208",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dorgan and Lott announce they 'will introduce legislation' to halt the FCC's media ownership rule changes. Per negative clarification (2), introducing or advocating pending legislation without claiming it has advanced or been adopted is position-taking, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4585": {
    "document_id": "4585",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Schumer announces that two Westchester County fire departments will receive over $134,000 in federal fire grants, part of nearly $3 million statewide. Per clarification (1), an announcement from the senator's office that federal funds are coming to his state counts as credit-claiming; the release also notes Schumer 'has pushed for additional funding' for the program, reinforcing his claimed role."
  },
  "4771": {
    "document_id": "4771",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release covers a meeting, it explicitly touts that in 2005 Clinton 'played a key role along with SMAC in preventing the removal of four C-130 Aircraft' at Stratton Air National Guard base, following the BRAC decision to keep the aircraft there. This claims personal responsibility for a specific concrete past outcome—saving a mission/aircraft at the base—which fits the definition ('saving or attracting a facility or mission') and clarification (5) (touting past achievements even in a release about a new initiative). This is a discrete win being touted, not merely general procedural involvement."
  }
}

{
  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The document contains only a bare title referencing a committee markup on venture capital and education bills, with no substantive text. There is no claim of a delivered outcome, no adopted amendment, no secured funding, and no assertion of personal responsibility for any governmental action. Absent any credit-claiming content, it defaults to 0."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement advocates for cameras in federal courtrooms, notes that he has cosponsored two pending bills (the Sunshine in the Courtroom Act and the Televising Supreme Court Proceedings Act), and criticizes the Bush administration's secrecy. Under negative clarification (2), cosponsoring/advocating pending legislation without claiming it advanced is not credit-claiming, and under (1) criticizing the administration is position-taking. No concrete delivered outcome is claimed."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter requesting that the Air Force clarify religious expression guidelines at the Academy. Under negative clarification (4), making requests/demands of agencies or officials is not credit-claiming. His statements that he was 'pleased' with the religious climate survey and the Superintendent's steps fall under negative clarification (3) — being pleased with an outcome without claiming a causal role."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a concrete agency action — Medicare waiving late-enrollment penalties for hurricane-affected seniors — and explicitly ties it to Landrieu's intervention: the headline states the 'Administrator finally responds to Sen. Landrieu's letter,' the text says the testimony 'responds to urgent calls for clarification' by Landrieu, and Landrieu says the agency 'recognized the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention.' This matches clarification (4): claims that the senator's letter/appeal produced an agency action (the examples explicitly include 'a waiver granted')."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Landrieu and Kerry are described as calling for local contracts in Katrina/Rita rebuilding and writing the Small Business Administration to speed up loss verification. Under negative clarification (4), making requests/demands of agencies is not credit-claiming; no outcome is announced or claimed as resulting from their actions."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a specific federal grant ($78,046) to an Ohio fire department and explicitly credits DeWine: he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure $650 million' for it, and the grant comes 'through Sen. DeWine's bill.' This fits clarification (1) (office announcement of federal funds coming to the state) and clarification (5) (touting past enacted achievements — authoring the law that created the program)."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a condolence statement on the death of Rep. Norwood. Under negative clarification (5), condolences and commemorations are not credit-claiming. The line 'I was proud to work with Rep. Norwood on several healthcare initiatives' is a generic reference to past collaboration within a condolence, not a claim of a specific delivered outcome."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement at the CHIP enrollment ceremony attributes credit to others — 'Chairman Baucus and Senator Rockefeller have worked tirelessly with Senators Grassley and Hatch' — and urges the President not to veto the bill. Under negative clarification (6), attributing credit to someone else is not credit-claiming, and pressuring the President is position-taking/request under (1) and (4). Reid claims no personal causal role in the bill's passage."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the headline frames a request for an expedited hearing (a demand, normally excluded under negative clarification 4), the release also juxtaposes the delegation's earlier appeal with a delivered agency action: 'Domenici and Representatives... asked Agriculture Secretary... and President Bush to approve any request from New Mexico for emergency aid,' followed directly by 'The USDA Farm Service Agency announced Friday that livestock producers in 20 New Mexico counties can qualify for low-interest emergency loans.' This sequencing implies the senators' appeal produced the loan availability, matching clarification (4) — the senator's appeal producing an agency action, with the codebook's examples explicitly including 'a loan program opened.' I classified as 1 based on this implied causal claim, though the connection is stated less explicitly than in the Lott positive example, which created some ambiguity."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig's office announces a concrete delivered benefit — expansion of VA clinic services in Lewiston, Idaho — and implies his advocacy produced it: he thanks VA officials 'for their willingness to hear my concerns and - more importantly - respond to the needs of the veterans.' This fits clarification (1) (announcement from the senator's office of a federal project/service coming to the state) and clarification (4) (the senator's appeal producing an agency action)."
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the main subject is the introduction of pending legislation (excluded under negative clarification 2), the release also states: 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape Sen. Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan, a little girl from Iraq... was able to come... to the United States for treatment.' This is an explicit claim that her intervention produced a concrete governmental result — casework/oversight success under the definition and clarification (4) — which qualifies as credit-claiming."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding for coqui frog control in Hawaii and explicitly claims personal responsibility: after OMB denied the request, 'I then went looking and found another federal funding source to at least begin the control and containment process.' This matches clarification (1) (announcement of federal funds coming to the state) and (2) (explicit first-person claim of securing the benefit)."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a Lieberman op-ed arguing for continued commitment in Iraq and against retreat. It is pure position-taking under negative clarification (1), and it attributes credit to President Bush ('It is to the credit of President Bush...') under negative clarification (6). No governmental outcome is claimed as the senator's accomplishment."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Beyond voting for S.4 (position-taking), the release states: 'The bill also contains McCaskill's amendment to provide whistleblower protection and collective bargaining rights for airport screeners' — in legislation the Senate just approved. Under clarification (3), claims that the senator's amendment was adopted or their provisions included in advancing legislation count as credit-claiming."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement supports Vice Admiral Allen's nomination at a Commerce Committee hearing, praises the outgoing and incoming Commandants, and discusses Coast Guard missions. She claims no delivered outcome and no personal responsibility for any governmental action; supporting a nominee and praising officials is position-taking under negative clarification (1), with elements of honoring others under (5)."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage (79-14) of the Farm Bill and states 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs' benefiting South Carolina research/training and that the bill 'includes a Graham backed provision' providing economic adjustment assistance to textile manufacturers. Under clarification (3), claims that the senator's backed provisions were included in advancing legislation are credit-claiming. The SC Agriculture Commissioner's praise for 'Senator Graham's efforts in securing passage of the Senate Bill' reinforces the credit claim."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage (52-40) of the FY2008 budget resolution and states it includes 'a provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund.' Under clarification (3), claims that the senator's authored/backed provisions were included in advancing or enacted legislation count as credit-claiming. While much of the release is Reed explaining his support (position-taking), the specific claim that his championed provision is in the passed budget ties him to a concrete delivered element."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions comments on TVA's board decision to pursue a nuclear license for Bellefonte, saying he is 'pleased' and 'applauds the Board for supporting this application,' while noting the issue 'has been a priority of mine.' Under negative clarification (3), welcoming/applauding an outcome without claiming a causal role is not credit-claiming, and under (7), describing an issue as one's priority without claiming to have produced the decision does not qualify. He asserts no intervention that caused TVA's vote."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker's offices announce a concrete federal decision benefiting Tennessee — DOT approval of nonstop Essential Air Service between Jackson and Cincinnati — and note the senators 'wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application,' implying their intervention contributed to the agency's decision. This fits clarification (1) (announcement from the senators' offices of a federal benefit coming to their state) and clarification (4) (the senators' letter tied to an agency action)."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release's main subject is the introduction of the pending R-HoPE Act (excluded under negative clarification 2), it touts Domenici's past enacted achievement: the bill extends 'provisions included in the MMA that Domenici first authored and offered as legislation in February 2003' — i.e., his authored provisions became law in the Medicare Modernization Act of 2003. Under clarification (5), touting past enacted achievements ('my earlier provisions were signed into law') counts as credit-claiming even when embedded in a release about a new initiative. This goes beyond the 'past procedural involvement' excluded by the negative clarification because it claims authorship of provisions actually enacted."
  }
}
